00:22:33,070 --> 00:22:36,896
So this seems to indicate things are not exactly going according to plan.

220
00:22:37,216 --> 00:22:41,060
We are producing code faster, but it's not necessarily better.

221
00:22:41,616 --> 00:22:41,936
Right?

222
00:22:42,096 --> 00:22:45,216
So this is the problem we we need to solve.

223
00:22:45,536 --> 00:22:49,988
Now, is there a nice solution around the corner?

224
00:22:51,086 --> 00:22:51,716
Hopefully.

225
00:22:53,966 --> 00:22:59,844
First thing to observe is that coding was never really the big part of the problem in the first place.

226
00:23:00,496 --> 00:23:09,412
in 1975, Fred Brooks wrote in the Mythical Man Month that one in six or roughly 17% of the effort is actually coding.

227
00:23:10,542 --> 00:23:21,456
And in 2025, a survey of developers found that 16% uh is coding and the rest you know other things.

228
00:23:21,616 --> 00:23:28,416
Um shockingly though, for 50 years apart, the effort that you put into coding didn't actually change.

229
00:23:28,576 --> 00:23:32,576
It's still basically one in six of the of the total effort.

230
00:23:32,896 --> 00:23:37,456
So what we're talking about here is shrinking, a very small part of the problem, in fact.

231
00:23:37,616 --> 00:23:44,896
Unfortunately, it's a really important part of the problem because if you make a mistake in in the coding part, then everything falls apart, everything breaks.

232
00:23:45,056 --> 00:23:48,576
So um it's small, but it's important.

233
00:23:49,262 --> 00:23:53,776
But what these numbers really say is you should focus more about the on the other parts now.

234
00:23:53,936 --> 00:23:58,916
Coding is not necessarily the part where we need to make all your contributions.

235
00:23:59,886 --> 00:24:04,576
So here we can start now with what can you actually control in this setting.

236
00:24:04,816 --> 00:24:06,816
First one is the specification, right?

237
00:24:07,056 --> 00:24:10,976
You need to be very precise about what you ask this agent to build.

238
00:24:11,216 --> 00:24:16,656
And uh uh you want the specification to be such that it's testable.

239
00:24:16,816 --> 00:24:19,748
So, for example, you don't want to say, I want it to look nice.

240
00:24:20,334 --> 00:24:22,096
You know, that's pretty useless, right?

241
00:24:22,176 --> 00:24:24,016
You can what it what does nice mean?

242
00:24:24,096 --> 00:24:25,616
How do you evaluate nice?

243
00:24:25,870 --> 00:24:30,896
This is not something that you can actually test for, so that's not a good requirement.

244
00:24:31,136 --> 00:24:32,612
Should be unambiguous.

245
00:24:33,198 --> 00:24:40,932
If you give one of these agents an ambiguous task, the odds are they'll pick exactly the one version of it you didn't want them to implement.

246
00:24:41,198 --> 00:24:41,568
Right.

247
00:24:41,728 --> 00:24:43,968
So no ambiguity.

248
00:24:44,128 --> 00:24:45,248
You want it to be consistent.

249
00:24:45,328 --> 00:24:48,128
So you don't want to say if this holds, then do that.

250
00:24:48,208 --> 00:24:50,528
And then later on say if this holds, do that.

251
00:24:50,848 --> 00:24:52,448
that's gonna confuse the agents.

252
00:24:52,608 --> 00:24:54,372
They're not, they're gonna pick one.

253
00:24:55,248 --> 00:24:57,408
and that's clearly not a good idea.

254
00:24:57,488 --> 00:24:58,528
And you want it to be complete.

255
00:24:58,608 --> 00:25:02,528
You don't want some scenario that's not covered by the agents.

256
00:25:02,848 --> 00:25:17,088
Um, a very interesting article, especially if you're involved at university, is this one at the bottom by um Christa Lopez, which is the anatomy of a learning stall, published in in June 26, I think it was on LinkedIn.

257
00:25:17,808 --> 00:25:34,048
it's all about a lot of your students, which is you give them a task and they just go run to the first gen.AI engine they can lay their hands on and come back with the result, and they actually have no idea what this solution is that this thing proposed to them.

258
00:25:34,350 --> 00:25:44,676
Uh and the quote there is correctness is not the same thing as it's not the same as the right thing, which means you can build a thing, but you need to know that you built the right thing.

259
00:25:45,134 --> 00:25:49,220
So you can build that thing correctly, but is it the thing you wanted to build?

260
00:25:51,808 --> 00:25:56,580
Okay, so now on to a few possible solutions to this problem.

261
00:25:56,928 --> 00:26:01,488
So the one thing that we're working a lot on is this concept of neurosymbolic reasoning.

262
00:26:01,808 --> 00:26:08,608
So AI basically has always had two components, the probabilistic side and the symbolic AI.

263
00:26:08,768 --> 00:26:16,100
And the difference between these two essentially is the probabilistic side will give you an answer with a certain probability.

264
00:26:16,528 --> 00:26:21,028
Whereas on the symbolic AI side, the answer is yes-no answers.

265
00:26:21,808 --> 00:26:23,728
That's the fundamental difference.

266
00:26:24,048 --> 00:26:34,128
But now, what the probabilistic side is good at, it's very kind of flexible in somewhat messy situations, right?

267
00:26:34,208 --> 00:26:36,128
Just like a large language model is today.

268
00:26:36,288 --> 00:26:42,848
It can do all kinds of things where you kind of don't necessarily specify exactly how to do it, etc. and so on.

269
00:26:43,568 --> 00:26:48,928
and it gives you plausible answers, sounds right, but it's not necessarily right.

270
00:26:49,088 --> 00:26:52,528
So this is kind of like the classic hallucination style problem.

271
00:26:52,688 --> 00:26:55,568
On the other side, you have the symbolic uh solution.

272
00:26:55,822 --> 00:27:01,888
This is typically based on logic, based on all this kind of decidable theories of these logics.

273
00:27:02,478 --> 00:27:08,768
you can actually give yes, no, or I don't know to be honest, is another option that these tools can give.

274
00:27:09,728 --> 00:27:13,124
but you need to be very precise about what it is you're specifying.

275
00:27:13,648 --> 00:27:21,028
So the combination of these two give you a way to translate the messy world into the formal world, and then the formal world does the checking.

276
00:27:21,328 --> 00:27:26,048
So to get from the messy to the formal world, you use the large language models.

277
00:27:26,368 --> 00:27:31,808
To find out if this is right or wrong, you use the actual symbolic reasoning engines.

278
00:27:31,968 --> 00:27:35,748
So the first place you can use this is in the specifications.

279
00:27:36,526 --> 00:27:42,852
So when you have a specification, first thing you want to do is you don't want to be vague, You don't want to say it should be nice.

280
00:27:43,150 --> 00:27:45,888
You want it to actually specify something you can test.

281
00:27:45,968 --> 00:27:51,492
So, for example, you want to say in a certain scenario, if I do this thing, then I want that outcome.

282
00:27:52,128 --> 00:27:55,408
That kind of specification is something you can test.

283
00:27:55,488 --> 00:28:00,036
So you want all your tests to be converted into forms that is easily testable, like that.

284
00:28:00,528 --> 00:28:04,048
Next thing you can do is you can take these requirements and you can then formalize them.

285
00:28:04,128 --> 00:28:07,876
So you can translate them into some kind of logic description.

286
00:28:08,048 --> 00:28:12,560
Now, with that logic description, you can look for conflicts, you can see gaps, et cetera.

287
00:28:12,622 --> 00:28:20,324
But the other thing you can do is you can ask various agents to basically formalize the same thing for you.

288
00:28:21,008 --> 00:28:28,048
And then you can take these results and you can bucket them into their logical equivalence classes.

289
00:28:28,208 --> 00:28:39,408
And if you get, for example, just one class at the end of this, you know your specification was pretty precise because all these models basically translated them into something that's logically equivalent.

290
00:28:39,648 --> 00:28:47,088
If, however, you get say two of these buckets, then you know it's vague because the LLMs couldn't decide that you mean A or B.

291
00:28:47,168 --> 00:28:51,088
And there's a little example here on the right where you say, I want to remove a record.

292
00:28:51,408 --> 00:28:58,208
Well, if you want to remove a record, do you want to completely delete it, or do you just want to mark it as deleted for some audit purpose?

293
00:28:58,448 --> 00:29:05,412
So the LLM will then just in a refinement step at the end, will ask you, did you mean this one or did you mean this one?

294
00:29:05,648 --> 00:29:09,808
And in that way, you can refine your specifications and get to to much more precise.

295
00:29:10,208 --> 00:29:17,924
And this stuff is all built into the the Kiro system that we have at AWS, and this is where the example also comes from.

296
00:29:20,608 --> 00:29:27,716
Next step you can do is you can take these requirement specifications and you can translate them into actual tests.

297
00:29:27,968 --> 00:29:33,168
So you have a spec on the left there and a piece of Python code in the middle there that'll actually do the test.

298
00:29:33,568 --> 00:29:37,764
But very important here is that these tests need to be evaluated by a human.

299
00:29:38,448 --> 00:29:46,564
Because I tried to simulate here in the middle that there's this one assertion in red that was actually added by the LLM that wasn't in the specification.

300
00:29:46,768 --> 00:29:51,968
And a human needs to be able to do that because the LLM is still going to hallucinate, it's still going to make these kind of mistakes.

301
00:29:52,048 --> 00:29:55,488
You do need to check these as a as a human.

302
00:29:55,808 --> 00:30:00,768
And a really important part is never generate the test from the actual code.

303
00:30:01,008 --> 00:30:03,008
Use the spec to generate the test.

304
00:30:03,328 --> 00:30:13,328
Because if you generate it from the code, all the LLM is doing is just verifying the code it wrote in the first place to just confirm the code, right?

305
00:30:13,488 --> 00:30:17,796
And not actually what the specification of the code is trying to do.

306
00:30:20,928 --> 00:30:32,260
Okay, the next step is just the basics, which is that when these agents generate code, the only way that they can do anything sensible is if they have feedback.

307
00:30:32,448 --> 00:30:34,052
And the more feedback, the better.

308
00:30:34,288 --> 00:30:34,448
Right?

309
00:30:34,608 --> 00:30:40,208
So the first feedback we already spoke about, which is the test, which is the require test based on the requirements.

310
00:30:40,288 --> 00:30:51,648
But you can augment the requirements test with some property-based tests, which could also be based off of the requirements, and of course fuzzing, which basically is blasted with random data and see what blows up.

311
00:30:51,888 --> 00:31:06,084
And those can enhance the test, but then you can also have bug finding tools like say a static analysis that looks for null pointer dereferences, or maybe some issues that you're leaking some memory or things like that.

312
00:31:07,566 --> 00:31:09,408
code coverage, of course, go with the test.

313
00:31:09,742 --> 00:31:13,412
Now you can also have things like the complexity metrics of the code.

314
00:31:13,808 --> 00:31:15,968
you can look at the architecture, for example.

315
00:31:16,128 --> 00:31:21,328
You can do now, you can build all kinds of agents that look at the code that you're you've written.

316
00:31:21,648 --> 00:31:25,892
like I said before, these agents can be spectacularly good at finding bugs.

317
00:31:27,182 --> 00:31:36,644
you might recall how Mythos found an unholy number of security issues in big companies' code, including Amazon.

